The following text is from Nalin Eren's 1942 poem “Bankside Argument.” The Embankment and the River speak as characters.

EMBANKMENT: <u>No sure defense but boundaries of stone;
Let mountain torrents batter where they will,
Each guarded field shall keep its harvest grown,
And every wandering channel shall be still.</u>
RIVER: But thirsty roots beyond your ramparts lie;
Your sheltered fields may watch their orchards die.
EMBANKMENT: Though scattered branches wither in the heat,
Hold fast the wall beneath the village street;
A measured bank makes every flood retreat.
